<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gwadar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gwadar is a port city on the Makran coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Balochistan</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, on a hammerhead peninsula facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>. Granted by the Khan of Kalat to an Omani prince in the 1780s, it remained an Omani possession until it was sold to Pakistan in 1958. Since the 2000s a deep-water port has been developed there with Chinese investment.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gwadar.jpg" alt="Gwadar" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shayhaq Baloch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gwadar_city,_the_doors_of_Air.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gwadar stands on a natural hammerhead-shaped tombolo peninsula, forming two curved semicircular bays on either side. The city lies on a narrow sandy isthmus 12 kilometres long and 240 metres wide, which joins the Pakistani coast to the rocky Gwadar Promontory, or Koh-e-Batil, rising to 150 metres. The eastern bay, the Demi Zirr, is deep and is where the port was built. The name combines the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochi-language">Balochi</a> words gwát, wind, and dar, gateway, so that Gwadar means the gate of wind. The climate is hot desert, moderated by the cool currents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>, with about 100 millimetres of rain a year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People have lived on this coast since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, around the area's oases, and the name Makran derives from this settlement pattern. It was for a time part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Persian Empire</a> and is believed to have been conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s march through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gedrosia">Gedrosia</a> in 325 BCE, his admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nearchus">Nearchus</a> led a fleet along the coast and described a dry, mountainous land inhabited by the Ichthyophagoi, the fish-eaters. The region came under Mauryan rule around 303 BCE, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a> made peace with Chandragupta and ceded the territory. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> captured Makran in 643 CE.</p><p>In 1783 Nasir Khan I, Khan of Kalat, handed Gwadar to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> bin Ahmad, a prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> who had fallen out with his brother, on the understanding that it would return to Kalat when the prince took the throne. He became ruler of Muscat in 1797 but never returned it, and the British helped Muscat keep Gwadar. From 1863 to 1879 it was the seat of a British Assistant Political Agent. Makran acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1947 without Gwadar, and on 8 September 1958 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> handed Gwadar over to Pakistan, after Prince Karim Aga Khan IV contributed about 3 million US dollars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The United States Geological Survey identified the site as suitable for a deep-water port in 1954, while it was still under Omani rule. Construction of the port began in 2007, and its first phase cost 248 million US dollars. The 653-kilometre Makran Coastal Highway, completed in 2004, links Gwadar with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> announced the China–Pakistan Economic Corridor in April 2015, with Gwadar at its centre. Before that, the economy rested largely on artisanal fishing. The population is predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baloch">Baloch</a>, and 96.2 per cent of the people of Gwadar tehsil speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochi-language">Balochi</a> as their mother tongue.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gwadar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>China calls it an 'Iron Brother': China is its largest trading partner and the builder of <b>Gwadar</b> port and the China–Pakistan Economic Corridor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The port of <b>Gwadar</b> on its coast belonged to Muscat until 1958.</blockquote><a href="/state/makran-state">Makran State</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gwadar</b> on the Makran coast was acquired in the 1780s and Bandar Abbas leased from Persia.</blockquote><a href="/state/omani-empire">Omani Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Said bin Taimur, sultan from 1932, kept the country isolated, won the Jebel Akhdar War of 1954-1959 and sold <b>Gwadar</b> to Pakistan in 1958.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-muscat-and-oman">Sultanate of Muscat and Oman</a></li></ul></section>
